      "scene": "Daren attacked Mira's report before the council. Soren watched with equanimity, a steadiness that did not depend on pleasant circumstances. He seemed imperturbable, difficult to upset, but Mira could see his fingers tighten. The governor remained impassive, revealing no emotion. A stolid guard stared ahead with almost dull indifference. Leela, naturally phlegmatic, calmly checked a measurement while the irascible chairman searched for another offense. His invective became personal abuse. A vituperative remark about the porter began a tirade against supposedly disloyal employees. Daren contributed a diatribe, a bitter denunciation of inexperienced clerks. The chairman's harangue then continued until the lamps needed trimming. Mira had a caustic answer ready. Soren touched the unopened ledger instead.",
      "price": "Mira lost her equanimity at the mention of the porter. Being imperturbable seemed less attractive than being heard. Her formerly impassive face changed, and even the stolid guard turned. The phlegmatic engineer waited while Mira challenged the irascible chairman's honesty. Soon her own invective competed with his. A vituperative exchange replaced the investigation. Her tirade contained true grievances, but listeners remembered its anger. Daren's next diatribe presented her reaction as proof that her work was unreliable. A second harangue exhausted the remaining audience. Her caustic sentence won a few smiles and lost the hearing's purpose. The ledger stayed closed. A person can be correct about the facts and still help another person bury them beneath a quarrel.",
      "countermove": "Before the next hearing, Mira practiced equanimity by answering hostile questions slowly. Soren warned that an imperturbable appearance was not the same as judgment. She need not look impassive when someone suffered, or become stolid to survive a meeting. Leela's phlegmatic method offered a better example: identify the next useful action. When the irascible chairman resumed his invective, Mira asked for the disputed receipt to be read aloud. She did not answer a vituperative label with another. A threatened tirade met a short request for evidence. Daren's diatribe lost momentum when she named its unanswered question. The harangue ended early. Her one caustic observation stayed in her notebook, where it could hurt neither the porter nor the case.",
      "beyond": "In an argument online, equanimity may mean waiting before posting. An imperturbable person can still care deeply; an impassive expression only tells you that emotion is not visible. A stolid response may reflect temperament or dullness, while a phlegmatic response suggests a calm disposition. An irascible person becomes angry readily. Their invective can tempt you into a vituperative reply. Soon a tirade about one mistake becomes a diatribe against an entire group. A long harangue may satisfy the speaker without answering the original question. A caustic joke can make repair harder. Before replying, decide whether you need correction, agreement, protection, or simply the last word. Those goals call for different actions.",
      "practice": "Think of equanimity as preserving your ability to choose. You need not become permanently imperturbable, visibly impassive, or emotionally stolid. Borrow the phlegmatic habit of attending to the immediate task when an irascible opponent raises the temperature. Identify invective as abuse rather than evidence. Refuse a vituperative exchange, shorten your own tirade, and remove the personal accusation from a draft diatribe. Ask whether your planned harangue will improve the decision. Save the caustic remark until you can judge its cost. Mira eventually apologized for her outburst without withdrawing her findings. That distinction gave her back the hearing. Composure mattered because it let the facts remain in the room after her anger passed."
